== Key Concepts ==

=== Control Areas ===
The fundamental geographic unit for this pipeline. Each control area has a unique <code>control_id</code>. Control-area (and control-HCT) geography is now built by the separate <code>control_total_geography</code> repo and manually uploaded to ElmerGeo (<code>CONTROL26</code>/<code>CONTROL_HCT26</code>); this repo reads the polygons from ElmerGeo rather than building them. Growth targets are set at the target-area level (groups of control areas), and results are distributed back to individual control areas.

=== Target Methodologies ===
Different counties use different approaches to define growth targets:
* '''Housing Unit Change''' (<code>unit_chg</code>): Targets specify change in housing units. Used by Kitsap (53035), Pierce (53053), and Snohomish (53061). Population, households, and household size are derived from housing unit changes combined with OFM vacancy rates and decennial household-size data.
* '''King County Method''' (<code>king_cnty_method</code>): Uses housing unit change targets but applies county-specific household sizes, vacancy rates, and a forced regional household population control total (2,828,620 for 2044).

=== RGID (Regional Growth ID) ===
A classification of target areas into Regional Growth categories. Used for aggregating and distributing employment and population targets, and for applying per-category parameters (e.g., King County household sizes, HCT split step values).

=== Resource and Construction Employment ===
King and Snohomish county employment targets '''exclude''' resource and construction sectors. These sectors are added back using a configured growth percentage (<code>res_con_emp_growth_pct</code> = 2.8%) distributed proportionally based on each target area's existing resource/construction share. Kitsap and Pierce targets '''include''' these sectors.

=== HCT Split ===
The HCT-split phase divides control totals into High Capacity Transit (HCT/TOD) and non-HCT components for each jurisdiction, targeting regional policy goals for transit-oriented growth (65% of households, 75% of employment in HCT areas). The HCT geography itself (<code>control_hct</code>/<code>CONTROL_HCT26</code>) is now built in the <code>control_total_geography</code> repo; this repo only reads it and spatially joins parcels to it (Step 4).

=== Subregional vs. Regional Control Totals ===
After the HCT split, growth is distributed into UrbanSim's control-total row format at two levels: '''subregional''' (Step 22, by <code>subreg_id</code> and PPH only) and '''regional''' (Step 23, region-wide by PPH, workers, and income). Both use the same underlying PPH-rebalancing algorithm ("Larry Blain's two-ratio formula", shared in <code>util.ct_allocation</code>) to iteratively fit household counts by PPH bin to each year's aggregate household/household-population control. The final output step (Step 24) merges subregional results where available with regional results as a fallback for years the subregional step didn't cover.

=== TOD / Subregion IDs ===
<code>subreg_id</code> identifies a HCT/TOD sub-area within a control area; for parcels in a TOD zone it equals <code>control_id + 1000</code> (matching the <code>chct_id</code> convention used by the <code>control_hct</code>/<code>CONTROL_HCT26</code> ElmerGeo layer), and equals <code>control_id</code> otherwise. This split geography lets growth be separately allocated to HCT and non-HCT sub-areas within each control area.

